---
// What people may reuse from Kinderform, and under which licence. Keep in step with README.md,
// LICENSE, LICENSE-CONTENT.md and THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md.
import Legal from "@/layouts/Legal.astro";

const h2 = "scroll-m-20 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight";
const p = "leading-7";
const ul = "ml-5 list-disc space-y-2 leading-7";
const link = "font-medium underline underline-offset-4";
const table = "w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm";
const th = "border-b px-2 py-2 align-top font-semibold";
const td = "border-b px-2 py-2 align-top leading-6";
const repo = "https://github.com/jamiegannaway/kinderform/blob/main";
const email = "jamie@kinderform.org";
---
<Legal
  title="Licences: what you can reuse from Kinderform"
  heading="Licences"
  description="Kinderform's code is under the MIT licence and its writing and data under CC BY 4.0. The drawings, Jamie's photograph and the Kinderform name are not open."
  current="/licences"
  updated="28 November 2026"
>
  <div slot="summary" class="space-y-4">
    <p class="text-xl leading-8 text-muted-foreground">
      You can reuse almost everything Kinderform makes, for free, for any purpose. Just give credit.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section aria-labelledby="open" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="open" class={h2}>What you can reuse</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class={table}>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col" class={th}>What</th>
            <th scope="col" class={th}>Licence</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class={td}>Code, including the site, the tools, the tests and the code in the starter kits</td>
            <td class={td}><a href={`${repo}/LICENSE`} class={link}>MIT licence</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class={td}>
              Writing and data: the enquiry standard (schema), the form wording, the registers, the census, the
              checklist, the research notes and the guides
            </td>
            <td class={td}>
              <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" class={link}>Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
              (CC BY 4.0)</a>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class={td}>Styles ported from KERN UX, DESY and NL Design System for the example forms</td>
            <td class={td}><a href={`${repo}/LICENSE-EUPL-1.2.txt`} class={link}>EUPL-1.2</a>, as their sources are</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class={td}>Fonts, icons, flags, the map and the design systems</td>
            <td class={td}>
              Their own open licences, listed in
              <a href={`${repo}/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md`} class={link}>third-party notices</a>
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class={p}>
      Under these licences, you can copy, change and share the work, including for commercial use. You must give credit
      and keep the licence notice.
    </p>
    <p class={p}>
      The licences cover reuse. They do not let automated tools collect from this site in any way they like. The
      <a href="/terms#automation" class={link}>terms of use</a> set the rules for crawlers and AI tools.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="credit" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="credit" class={h2}>How to give credit</h2>
    <p class={p}>
      When you share our writing or data, or your changed version of it, write:
    </p>
    <p class="rounded-lg border p-4 leading-7">
      "Based on Kinderform (kinderform.org) by Jamie Gannaway and Kinderform contributors, CC BY 4.0." Say if you changed
      it.
    </p>
    <p class={p}>
      When you share our code, keep the MIT licence notice with it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="forms" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="forms" class={h2}>Your service's live enquiry form needs no credit</h2>
    <p class={p}>
      If your service builds a live enquiry form with our wording, you do not need to show a credit on the form itself.
      Jamie gives this extra permission for the work he owns.
    </p>
    <p class={p}>
      Keep the credit when you share a kit, the wording or your changed version with other people or services.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="closed" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="closed" class={h2}>What you cannot reuse</h2>
    <ul class={ul}>
      <li>
        <strong>The drawings.</strong> They come from Notioly and we use them under a paid licence. Do not copy them.
        Buy your own licence from Notioly.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Jamie's photograph</strong> on the About page. It shows him and his family. It belongs to him, and all
        rights are reserved.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Kinderform name and logo.</strong> You can use the name to give credit. Do not use it to suggest we
        made, approved or support your work.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Other people's work we quote or link to</strong>, such as research reports and services' own pages. It
        belongs to its owners.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Government branding.</strong> The example forms leave out crests, logos and official banners. Do not add
        them back unless you are allowed to use them.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="facts" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="facts" class={h2}>Facts about services</h2>
    <p class={p}>
      Our registers record facts from services' public pages, with links. Our way of collecting and arranging them is
      under CC BY 4.0. The services' own words and pages stay theirs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="warranty" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="warranty" class={h2}>No guarantee</h2>
    <p class={p}>
      Everything is offered "as is", without any guarantee, as each licence says. Read our
      <a href="/terms" class={link}>terms of use</a> before you rely on it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ask" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="ask" class={h2}>Questions</h2>
    <p class={p}>
      If you are not sure whether you can reuse something, email
      <a href={`mailto:${email}`} class={link}>{email}</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Legal>
